# Auto-push after code changes

When the user asks you to **implement, fix, build, refactor, or update** code in this repo, finish by committing and pushing to GitHub unless they explicitly say not to.

## When this applies

- User requested code changes and you edited project files
- The task is complete (or you are stopping for the turn with work done)

## When to skip

- User only asked a question or review with no code changes
- User explicitly said "don't commit", "don't push", or "no git"
- There are no relevant changes to commit
- Changes include secrets (`.env`, credentials) — warn the user instead of committing

## Required workflow (end of implementation tasks)

1. Run `git status` and `git diff` to review changes
2. Stage only relevant files (never secrets or accidental junk)
3. Commit with a clear message focused on **why** (match recent `git log` style)
4. Push to the current branch: `git push -u origin HEAD` (or `git push` if already tracking)
5. Tell the user the commit hash and that the push succeeded

## Git safety

- Never `git push --force` to `main`/`master`
- Never update git config
- Never skip hooks unless the user explicitly requests it
- If push fails (auth, conflicts), report the error and what the user should do

## If you cannot push

Say clearly that changes are committed locally but not pushed, and include the exact error.
